  "notes": [
    "This is NOT a Windows measurement. Both builds ran natively on macOS, so the Windows runtimes (MSYS2 and Cygwin, their select() event loop and fork emulation) are not part of these numbers; Windows figures come from the Verify workflow's benchmark job.",
    "It is still indicative: both arms are the same Redis 8.10.2 source built with the same compiler, and they differ only in the optimization level (-O0 vs -O2) and in the patch series, which is portable C. Those are the same differences the Windows builds have.",
    "Stock: make MALLOC=libc CFLAGS=\"-Wno-char-subscripts -O0\". Fork: patches/redis applied with scripts/apply-patches.sh, then make MALLOC=libc OPTIMIZATION=-O2 CFLAGS=\"-Wno-char-subscripts\".",
    "ci/bench.sh run as the Verify workflow runs it: baseline and fork interleaved, one repetition each per round, 3 rounds, script defaults (BENCH_POPULATE_MB \"64 256\"). Each figure is the median of 3 repetitions; BGSAVE figures are the median of 9 saves (3 per repetition).",
    "Other applications were running on the machine throughout (load average 10.97 at the start and 8.02 at the end, on 14 logical CPUs). Single-client and large-value workloads run for well under a second each and are the most exposed to that noise."
  ]
